$DIR/test.p:3:12: f has no results, so no error result to carry err: return with no operands would drop the error
    An unbraced return with no operands in an or clause returns err as the
    function's last result. This function has no result of type error last, so
    there is nowhere to put err, and returning without it would drop the error.
    Handle the error in a block clause, or return values that this function has.
    The short form of return exists to pass an error up. In a function that
    cannot return one, the program must say what to do with the error.
    Handle the error in a block clause:
        load(path) or {
            log.Printf("loading: %v", err)
            return
        }
    (see "Or clauses" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.27)
    (run "pluto explain E2464" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/test.p:7:12: g has no error result to carry err: its last result has type int, and return with no operands would drop the error
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2464" for more)
